  "query_preview": "..\nIssued 300 PM CDT Fri Oct 9 2026\n\nSunday through Friday:\n\nClearing skies from west to east into Sunday, as remnants of \nIsaias move eastward into the Northeast, with highs in the \nupper 70s to lower 80s expected (highest temperatures in the \nwest where skies clear earliest). Southerly winds continue, but \nlighter as the low to the west weakens and lifts northward. \nOvernight, expect lows in the upper 50s to lower 60s.\n\nPattern begins to break down Monday through the week, with \nbroad high pressure over the Southeast and multiple shortwaves \npropagating over the northern Plains into the northern Great \nLakes. Isolated to scattered showers and storms are possible \nMonday afternoon and night (20 to 40 percent) as a stronger \nshortwave interacts with a broad warm frontogenesis region. \nHighs in the upper 70s to lower 80s and lows in the 50s are\nexpected. A brief period of dry conditions expected Tuesday \ninto Tuesday night, as high pressure builds in behind the \npassing shortwave/cold front combo, with northerly winds. \nExpect highs in the 60s, lows in the 40s. \n\nBroad low pressure persists over the central Plains Wednesday \nthrough Friday, with its warm front expanding eastward across \nthe southern Great Lakes region into Wednesday afternoon.\nScattered showers and storms and increasing southeasterly winds\nare expected. Low pressure ejects into the Upper Great Lakes \nThursday into Thursday night, bringing widespread rain/storm \npotential. Low pressure exits to the northeast into Friday, with\na cold front sweeping through the region and bringing gusty \nnorthwesterly winds Friday into Friday night. Lows in the 30s \nare possible Friday night, and isolated pockets of frost may \ndevelop.\n\nMH",
